  "plan": {
    "goal": "Recapitulate the very low physiological fluid shear of the intestinal lumen on a Caco-2 monolayer in a perfused microchannel.",
    "rationale": "The intestinal lumen experiences very low wall shear (≈0.002–0.02 Pa, i.e. 0.02–0.2 dyn/cm²), far below vascular values. I target ~0.01 Pa as a mid-range physiological gut-lumen shear. A wide, shallow channel (1000 µm × 150 µm) mimics the broad, thin lumen geometry and, at 2.25 µL/min, yields 0.01 Pa wall shear (verified via wall_shear_stress). The flow is deeply laminar (Re ≈ 0.065), residence time ≈ 80 s, and pressure drop ≈ 2.7 Pa — all well within syringe-pump and PDMS-device limits. Caco-2 seeding density 7.5e4 cells/cm² (within the 5e4–1e5 registry range) over a 0.2 cm² channel floor gives ~1.5e4 cells. Perfused O2 delivery at maximal extraction is ~0.45 nmol/min, comfortably above Caco-2 demand at this cell number.",
